WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.133
-Darren, a Brooklyn boy.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.667
-Yeah, I'm a beginner at this.

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.367
I've never done a talk show.

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:07.367
-This is the first talk show?
Oh, I'm so happy.

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:08.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.167
Welcome! Welcome, welcome!

00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:11.901
I'm so happy to have you here.
-Thank you.

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:14.601
-I have a thousand questions for
you, but we'll get right to it.

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:18.334
A director always
in your vision, growing up?

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:21.033
-I didn't know what a director
was growing up in Brooklyn.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:23.868
It was, like, no clue.
It was a different time.

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.601
-When did you get the bug
or go like,

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:28.000
"Hey, this might be something
that I'm good at"?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.767
-I actually went to King's Plaza
in Brooklyn,

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:31.934
for any Brooklyn people,

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:34.501
and the film I wanted to see
was sold out.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.067
And there was, like, another
film with a kind of goofy guy

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:39.601
with a Brooklyn hat,
and I walked into Spike Lee's

00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:41.033
"She's Gotta Have It."

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.334
And it was just like, I had
never seen anything like that.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.100
So it's sort of like,
"Oh, someone from Brooklyn

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.934
can do something in this world."

00:00:46.934 --> 00:00:48.734
And it kind of opened my eyes.
-Yeah.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.467
And then you go, "Okay,
so I'm going to do that."

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:53.234
And then you go --
What happens next?

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:56.400
-Well, I sort of followed a path
back then.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.234
There weren't, like,
film schools everywhere.

00:00:58.234 --> 00:00:59.734
So I kind of had to
figure it out.

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:02.334
And eventually I was like,

00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:04.601
"What is the cheapest thing
I can make?"

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:08.100
I raised money going out to
everyone I knew,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.934
asking for 100 bucks each
with the promise

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:12.367
if the film made money,
they'd get 150 bucks back.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:13.400
That was the deal.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.734
-That's what a Brooklyn kid
does.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.834
"Give me 100 bucks,
I'll give you 150."

00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:21.000
-Early crowdfunding.
I should have done Kickstarter.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.868
-Pre-Kickstarter, yeah.
And that movie was --

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:26.434
-"Pi."
-Yeah, not too shabby.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:29.167
-Which it's actually the
25th anniversary this year,

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:32.701
and we're actually scanning it
for IMAX,

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:34.934
and we're going to release it
on Pi Day, March 14th,

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.567
for the world to see it again.
-Oh, my God.

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:38.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
Not too shabby.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:41.033
-Yeah, not too shabby.
-Not too shabby.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.167
-I also just saw you're on
the short list --

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.067
You have a documentary film
called "The Territory."

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:46.801
You're on the short list to be
nominated for an Oscar.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.067
So that's good news.
Congratulations.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:50.767
Good luck on that.
What is "The Territory" about?

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.868
-"Territory" is
an amazing documentary.

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:54.467
Probably one of the most
important things

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.067
I've ever been lucky to be near.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:02:00.400
And it documents kind of
the front lines of the war

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.534
happening in the Amazon between
the indigenous people

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:04.300
and people that are invading
and taking their lands.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.968
It's on Disney+.
It's really badass.

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:08.667
-Alright, congratulations.
That's fantastic.

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:11.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
So, that's happening.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:13.801
You do that.
And then you have "The Whale."

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.501
-Yeah.
-Which you directed.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.934
Brendan Fraser.
He's unbelievable in this.

00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:20.734
Sadie Sink.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:22.334
-Sadie Sink is unbelievable
as well.

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:24.267
When did you hear about
this idea?

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.934
When did you go,
"I want to make this"?

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:27.267
And how long did it take you?

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:30.000
-Well, it was originally a play
that I saw 10 years ago,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.601
off-Broadway theater,

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:33.200
Playwrights Horizons here
in New York.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.300
And I was just so deeply
moved by it.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.133
It was characters that I had
never met before.

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:41.267
I shouldn't be able to relate
to them, but by the end,

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.200
I was like -- I felt so deeply,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.300
and I was crying with all these
strangers in the audience.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.133
And I was like, "It would be
really interesting

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.634
to bring that to the theater"
'cause people don't cry

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:51.033
anymore in movies, you know.

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:54.100
That used to be a thing back
in the day, the tear-jerker.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.934
-That was my first movie, I
think, was "The Champ."

00:02:55.934 --> 00:02:57.100
-"The Champ"
with Ricky Schroder.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.300
There you go, exactly.
-Cried my eyes out.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.200
-People used to brag about
crying in theaters.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.767
-So you're bringing the cry
back?

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:06.234
-#CryTogether.
-#CryTogether.

00:03:06.234 --> 00:03:08.968
You're bringing it back.
Why not? Family get-together.

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.334
Have a good cry.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:13.934
-But it is inspirational
and hopeful.

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:15.400
I mean, it leaves you with --

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.801
The character has so much life
and so much hope for humanity.

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:21.767
-Also, Brendan is a perfect
choice because he's funny.

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:24.467
He knows how to deliver a joke
and land a joke.

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:27.968
Did you know that Brendan was
going to be the main character?

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.901
-Well, the reason it took
10 years was

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:32.334
I couldn't figure out
how to cast it.

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:36.534
And then I stumbled on a trailer
of a low-budget Brazilian movie,

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:40.200
and Brendan had a small role
in it and he's amazing.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.501
To be honest though, I had never
really seen any of his films.

00:03:43.501 --> 00:03:46.334
I hadn't seen "George of
the Jungle" or "Encino Man."

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:48.434
I had seen "The Mummy"
but not that much.

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:51.400
But there was something that,
like, a light bulb went off.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.234
And I was like,
"Let's roll the dice on it."

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.968
-And did you see a scene
in your head?

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.200
Did you go, "I think he's going
to do this scene pretty --"

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.501
-It wasn't that.
It was just really, um...

00:04:00.501 --> 00:04:03.601
You know, I think once a movie
star, always a movie star,

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:06.501
and there was a time
when people just loved him.

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:08.067
-Yeah.
-But I had no idea about this

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.901
Brenaissance and that people
would be going crazy over him.

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:14.501
-Yeah, Brenaissance, exactly.

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:16.334
He got a 10-minute
standing ovation.

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:17.734
-Yeah, yeah.
-After the premiere.

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:22.167
I mean, just that clip went
viral because he stood up

00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:23.567
and he was losing it.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:24.734
This is not even in the movie.

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:26.567
He's crying after the movie.
He's crying.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:27.968
I'm crying watching him.

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:29.367
I think you got
a little emotional.

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:33.033
-Yeah, yeah, it was very --
I've had a few films

00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:35.234
at the Venice Film Festival and
this one was very different.

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:37.934
It was really unique because
the love for him was great.

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:40.367
And it's just been so nice
to see people

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.801
loving what he's doing
in this movie

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.734
and so excited that he's back.
-And rooting for him

00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:45.767
and rooting for the movie,
and it pays off.

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:48.567
How do we set up "The Whale"
for anyone?

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.834
-It's a hard one for me.
I hate doing this thing.

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:52.601
But it's like, um...
-I can try to help you.

00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:53.667
-Please.

00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:57.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.801
-It is a, uh...
I'd say it's kind of like

00:04:59.801 --> 00:05:03.100
a broken family almost.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.200
-And he is a teacher,
and he's trying to reconnect

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.100
with his daughter, who he hasn't
seen in a while,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.601
and teenage girls, and the whole
thing is tough,

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:14.234
and it's a lot of emotion
that comes out

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:18.167
and he's trying to say, "Hey, I
made mistakes but I love you."

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:21.133
And they're trying to
communicate with each other.

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:22.801
-That's wonderful.
-Is that okay?

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:23.801
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:25.067
-My God, that was
a lot of pressure.

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:27.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:29.868
It's great and it pays off
and it's funny as well.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:31.067
You'll get some cries in there

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:32.534
but you'll get some laughs
as well.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.868
I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's Brendan Fraser in a scene

00:05:34.868 --> 00:05:37.133
from Darren Aronofsky's
"The Whale." Take a look.

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:43.801
-I need to know that she's going
to have a decent life

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:49.400
where she cares about people
and other people care about her

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.334
and she's going to be okay.

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:54.133
-I need to go.
-Mary, she doesn't...

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.300
-I have to go.
-...have anyone else.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:03.367
I need to know that I have done
one thing right with my life!

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:07.701
-Oof.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:08.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.701
Darren Aronofsky, everyone.

00:06:10.701 --> 00:06:12.834
"The Whale" is in theaters now.

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:15.300
Emma Myers joins us after the
break. Stick around, everybody.

